"b": "The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) is the most comprehensive mandatory sustainability reporting regime in the world.",
"x": [
"The Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD) is the most comprehensive mandatory sustainability reporting regime in the world. Adopted in December 2022, it replaced the 2014 Non-Financial Reporting Directive and requires companies to report against the European Sustainability Reporting Standards (ESRS), a single set of twelve standards covering climate, pollution, water, biodiversity, workforce, communities, consumers and governance.",
"Its defining feature is double materiality. Companies must report not only how sustainability issues affect their finances, but also how their own activities affect people and the planet. Reports sit in the management report, are digitally tagged and are subject to external assurance, putting sustainability information on a similar footing to financial information.",
"The first companies reported on financial year 2024. Business pushback over cost and complexity then led the EU to scale the regime back. The Omnibus I Directive, in force since March 2026, limits mandatory reporting to companies with more than 1,000 employees and over €450 million in turnover, and protects smaller firms in the value chain from excessive data requests. In July 2026 the Commission adopted revised ESRS that cut mandatory data points by more than 60%, applying from 2027.",
"Why it matters. The CSRD shows that governments can make impact reporting, and not only financial risk reporting, a legal obligation, and that standards can be built to work alongside the global ISSB baseline. Its recent narrowing is equally instructive: ambition must be matched by proportionality, clear guidance and a realistic timetable if a disclosure regime is to survive contact with the companies it covers. The revised framework remains the reference point for impact-oriented disclosure worldwide."
],
"src": "GSG Impact Policy Tracker; CMS, 'Final adoption of Omnibus I Directive' (March 2026); Rödl & Partner, 'Revised ESRS as a delegated act' (July 2026)",
"url": "https://www.impactpolicytracker.org/sustainability-disclosures/CSRD+ESRS.html"
